  <h2 id="results">Submitted Minibinder Results</h2>
  <p>The expandable table contains all 35 designs submitted across the two campaigns, with their reported computational scores and assay outcomes. In total, one design had confirmed binding, 18 received negative calls, and 16 did not yield a usable binding call. Missing scores remain unreported.</p>
  <div class="wide"><DesignResults client:visible /></div>
  <p>Search or filter the table to compare designs, select a column heading to sort, and expand a design row for additional metrics. Three submitted designs have matching structures in the viewer and link directly to those models. The viewer also contains other predicted complexes; a model's presence there does not establish experimental binding.</p>
  <p>Assay outcomes come from the overall Round 1 and Round 2 BLI records. Computational metrics are matched by design ID to the campaign report, final 20-design record, consensus export, and validated panel. The combined assay fit is not counted as an additional replicate.</p>

  <h1 id="implications">Implications for SWARM</h1>
  <p>The modeling work supported the identification of an experimentally confirmed DWV-binding minibinder. It also exposed limitations that shaped our interpretation: biological assembly context matters, sequence properties require consideration, and repeated predictions can reveal uncertainty concealed by a single confidence score.</p>
  <p>The measured binder provides a starting point for further characterization. Its binding affinity alone does not establish viral neutralization or colony-level protection. Those questions connect this work to <a href="/lambert-ga/minibinder-experimentation/">minibinder experimentation</a> and the <a href="/lambert-ga/model-minibinder/">minibinder dose model</a>.</p>

  <h1 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h1>
  <p>Across two campaigns, computational predictions and experimental measurements provided different but complementary evidence. The initial campaign showed that high confidence scores could coexist with unsuccessful experimental outcomes. The second campaign identified a 79-amino-acid minibinder with a measured K<sub>D</sub> of approximately 604 nM. Together, these results demonstrate the value of evaluating modeling decisions against experimental data while retaining the uncertainty and limitations of both.</p>
  <p>This page is adapted from the team's <a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6185/wiki/lastmin/modeling-main-protein-modeling.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Main Protein Modeling writeup (PDF)</a>, with submitted-design outcomes checked against the campaign records.</p>
</InformationLayout>
